+#### Baking Neural Fields (SNeRG)
+- **Definition:** Uses a hierarchical data structure where the coarsest level is a $(N / B)^3$ resolution dense voxel grid pointing to blocks of a size-$B^3$ dense voxel grid, making use of sparsity while keeping data contiguous [S1, unofficial]. It decomposes an image into diffuse color (view-independent) and specularity (view-dependent) so the inference network handles a simple task [S1, unofficial].
+- **Step:** Computes weighted sum of intermediate features and then evaluates color per pixel, significantly reducing the number of feed forward passes compared to evaluating color per 3D sample [S1, unofficial].
+- **Pitfall:** Octrees' intermediate non-leaf nodes and tree traversals incur significant memory and time overhead, which this method avoids by using block pointers [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of explicit radiance fields originated with the need to accelerate NeRF training and rendering. While NeRF used multi-layer perceptrons to implicitly represent scenes, explicit methods store scene properties directly in memory structures. PERF (Explicit Radiance Fields) was one of the first to use a Gauss-Newton solver for explicit voxel grids [S1]. Plenoxels later popularized the use of sparse voxel grids with spherical harmonics for view-dependent color, achieving significant speedups [S1][S7, unofficial]. Subsequent works like PlenOctree and Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization refined these approaches with hierarchical structures and sparse memory management [S1, unofficial][S1].
+Originating from the need to accelerate NeRF training and inference, explicit radiance fields like Plenoxels and PERF introduced voxel-based representations to replace implicit neural networks [S1, S2, S4, S5]. The use of spherical harmonics for view-dependent color and analytical gradients for optimization allows for orders-of-magnitude speedups [S1, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The family has evolved to include sparse representations, hierarchical pruning, and multi-scale approaches to handle unbounded scenes and complex geometry [S1, S2, S4, S5].
 
